WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:33.167
Record outgoing work. A booking succeeds and records a receipt job reliably. Coordinate this with business storage, for example using an outbox, so a crash does not lose the obligation.

2
00:00:33.167 --> 00:01:07.000
Buffer a burst. The queue stores work while consumers catch up. Stored backlog buys time, but retention, storage and acceptable delay remain finite.

3
00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:47.917
Process within limits. Workers pull jobs at a safe rate. Their combined concurrency must respect email provider quotas and other downstream capacity.

4
00:01:47.917 --> 00:02:24.583
A worker crashes. A worker can send an email and fail before acknowledging its job. The queue may deliver that same logical job again.

5
00:02:24.583 --> 00:02:57.500
Protect the effect. Use stable job identity and an appropriate idempotency or reconciliation strategy. A delivery guarantee alone does not guarantee exactly one external effect.

6
00:02:57.500 --> 00:03:40.250
Handle persistent failure. After bounded attempts, move a poison job to a monitored dead-letter path. Investigate and repair before controlled replay instead of retrying forever.
